    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Mediate between peers in conflict. Communication: Clarify misunderstood social cues in peer interactions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can mediate a simple disagreement between peers about a favorite movie, using gestures to show listening and taking turns (e.g., hand gestures for "wait" and "listen").
Can clarify misunderstood social cues, using facial expressions and gestures to show understanding (e.g., nodding to confirm a peer's idea when clarifying opinions).
Can mediate discussions about movie genres, using gestures to emphasize different types (e.g., showing "big" hands for an action movie and "quiet" for a drama).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch two short video clips (1 minute each) of different movie genres (e.g., a thrilling action scene and a quiet drama scene). In pairs, students briefly discuss which movie type they prefer and why, using phrases like "I prefer action movies over dramas because they are more thrilling." The teacher then introduces a hypothetical scenario: "Imagine your two friends disagree about which movie was better. How can you help them understand each other?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simple mediation phrases and gestures. For example, "So, if I understand correctly, you think... (nodding, turning to one student), and you, (name), feel that... (nodding, turning to the other student)." The teacher also models gestures for "wait" (hand up) and "listen" (hand to ear) to facilitate turn-taking. Pronunciation of verb endings like "watches" /ɪz/ and "runs" /z/ can be reinforced when describing character actions or movie themes (e.g., "The main character watches the world change," "He runs away.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of mediation phrases and gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'So, if I understand correctly,' am I asking for more information or giving my opinion?" (Expected answer: Asking for more information/clarifying).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does this gesture (teacher shows hand up for 'wait') mean when someone is talking?" (Expected answer: To wait or let someone finish).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between three students discussing their comparative book reviews. Two students have slightly different opinions on a particular book's plot or character, and the third student mediates. The dialogue will include key mediation phrases and target vocabulary (movie, book, character, plot, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional, enjoy, prefer) and grammar (present simple for preferences, past simple for plot summary). Register is informal, conversational.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participating in discussions and providing constructive feedback in peer presentations and discussions on book reviews.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main problem the three students are trying to solve?" (Expected answer: They have different opinions about a book/movie review and need help understanding each other).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the dialogue and mark them true or false (e.g., "One student thinks the book's plot was boring. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match mediation phrases from the dialogue to their function (e.g., "What I hear you saying is..." -> summarizing an idea).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation phrases for clarifying, summarizing, and promoting turn-taking. Grammar: Present simple (preferences, reasons, verb + -ing + noun + because), Past simple (describing events/plot), Comparing preferences ("I prefer X over Y because..."). Vocabulary: movie, book, character, plot, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional, enjoy, prefer, express, describe, analyze, reflect.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences with appropriate mediation phrases. For example: "If I _____ you correctly, you mean you like reading fiction books because they are exciting?" or "What I hear you _____ is that the movie was very funny." The teacher provides a word bank of phrases like "understand," "saying," "explain," "clarify."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled practice (Rephrasing Opinions):</b> In pairs, one student gives an opinion about a book/movie using target grammar (e.g., "I enjoyed watching that documentary because it was so informative about its theme."). The other student practices mediating by rephrasing the opinion using a mediation phrase (e.g., "So, if I understand correctly, you enjoyed the documentary because its theme taught you a lot?"). Students use gestures like nodding to confirm understanding. The teacher also emphasizes the pronunciation of contrastive vowel sounds like /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool" when students discuss movie full-length versions or "foolish" characters.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Mediating Preferences):</b> Students work in groups of three. Two students are given contrasting opinions about different movie genres (e.g., Student A: "I think action movies are the best because they are thrilling!" Student B: "I prefer documentaries because they make me reflect and learn!"). The third student practices mediating their disagreement, using gestures to show listening and taking turns, and phrases to clarify each peer's preference and reasons. The mediator must ensure both students feel heard.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative role-play and presentation of the "Comparative Book Review" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students work in groups of three. Two students briefly present their "Comparative Book Review" (prepared in Lesson 4), focusing on their preferred book/movie and justifying their choice using present simple and past simple (e.g., "I prefer this movie because it's more engaging. The plot described a journey..."). The third student acts as a mediator. Their role is to facilitate understanding if the other two students have different preferences or misunderstand each other's points. The mediator must clarify misunderstood social cues, rephrase opinions, ensure fair turn-taking, and help find common ground or acknowledge differences respectfully. They should use facial expressions (e.g., nodding to confirm) and gestures (e.g., showing "big" hands for an action movie) to emphasize and clarify. This activity allows students to provide constructive feedback to their peers. Sequencers (first, next, after, finally) can be used by presenters, and mediators can clarify points in the sequence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in using mediation strategies, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion (successfully mediating a discussion about comparative reviews).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the performance stage, the teacher uses an observation checklist to evaluate the mediator's effectiveness. Peers also provide feedback to the mediator using a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., movie, book, character, plot, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional, prefer) correctly integrated into explanations/clarifications.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of words and attention to /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings and vowel sounds like /ʊ/ and /uː/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in mediation:</b> Ability to rephrase ideas, clarify misunderstandings, and summarize points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and turn-taking:</b> Effective use of gestures (e.g., hand for "wait," nodding) and phrases to manage the discussion and ensure everyone participates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of present simple for preferences and reasons, and past simple for describing story elements.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short self-reflection:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new mediation phrase or gesture you used today to help someone understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you feel when you helped your peers clarify their opinions about their book/movie reviews?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about mediating a discussion about comparative reviews today, and what strategy helped you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use mediation skills outside of the classroom when your friends have different opinions about something?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this mediation lesson? Students were engaged in using gestures and phrases to clarify. The connection to the "Comparative Book Review" project made the mediation task relevant. Students showed good understanding of turn-taking. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still struggle with spontaneously producing complex mediation phrases; they tend to stick to simpler ones. More practice with summarizing longer statements might be needed. Reinforcing the /ʊ/ and /uː/ vowel sounds in context was somewhat challenging due to the fast pace of discussion. I might need to explicitly pause and drill those sounds in the next lesson if they are relevant.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Books and Movies" theme by allowing students to apply all previous language skills in a communicative, mediation-focused task. The next theme will build on communicative competence by introducing new scenarios requiring discussion and clarification, further developing their ability to express and mediate opinions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>